model NQueens

-- Classic N-Queens combinatorial puzzle, chosen specifically as a hard
-- SAT-solver stress test that scales with N: place N queens on an NxN
-- board so that no two attack each other (no shared row, column, or
-- diagonal). Demonstrating solver PERFORMANCE as N grows (not just
-- correctness) is the whole point of this example -- see
-- NQueens.properties for the two named sections ([small]/[large]) and
-- validate.cmd for their measured solving times, actually run against the
-- built plugin.
--
-- MODELING NOTE, discovered empirically while building this example (an
-- own mistake, not a plugin limitation -- recorded here so it isn't
-- repeated): a first, more "obvious" encoding -- a single Queen class with
-- row:Integer and col:Integer attributes directly, plus one invariant
-- requiring all four of (row-distinct, col-distinct, main-diagonal-
-- distinct, anti-diagonal-distinct) pairwise for every two queens --
-- reproducibly came back UNSATISFIABLE from the Kodkod solver for every
-- N>=4 actually tried (4, 6, 8, 10), even though a hand-built object
-- diagram satisfying that exact same OCL invariant was independently
-- confirmed valid via `check -v` (not just eyeballed) -- i.e. solutions
-- genuinely exist but the solver reported none. Bisecting which conjuncts
-- triggered it (splitting the invariant apart one comparison at a time)
-- pointed at the diagonal terms specifically; the actual root cause turned
-- out to be our own `Integer_min`/`Integer_max` bounds in that first
-- properties file: sized to cover row/col's own domain (0..N-1) but too
-- narrow for the *derived* diagonal sum (row+col ranges up to 2*(N-1)).
-- This plugin's Kodkod translation of `Integer` apparently needs
-- Integer_min/max to cover the full range of every arithmetic result the
-- model computes, not just each attribute's own declared domain --
-- otherwise the formula silently comes back UNSATISFIABLE instead of
-- erroring. Widening the bounds accordingly (see NQueens.properties) fixed
-- it at every N subsequently tried, without changing the invariant at all.
--
-- The model actually shipped below additionally moves row/col off Queen's
-- own attributes and onto two small helper classes (Row, Col) linked via
-- mandatory 1-1 associations, so that:
--   - "each queen gets a distinct row" and "each queen gets a distinct
--     column" are enforced STRUCTURALLY, by the association
--     multiplicities together with matching Row/Queen and Col/Queen
--     population counts (a total, mandatory 1-1 association between two
--     classes of equal fixed size IS a bijection) -- not by an extra
--     pairwise OCL distinctness invariant.
--   - the only OCL arithmetic invariant left on Queen is the diagonal
--     check itself (`Queen::noAttack`), which is exactly what this
--     example is measuring solver performance on as N grows.

class Row
attributes
  idx : Integer  -- this Row's board row number, 0..N-1
end

class Col
attributes
  idx : Integer  -- this Col's board column number, 0..N-1
end

class Queen
end

------------------------------------------------------------------------
-- Mandatory 1-1 on both ends, combined with matching Row/Queen and
-- Col/Queen population counts (set identically in NQueens.properties),
-- together force each association into a genuine bijection: every queen
-- gets exactly one row and one column, and every row/column is used by
-- exactly one queen -- "one queen per row, one queen per column" for
-- free, with no arithmetic distinctness invariant required for it.

association QueenRow between
  Queen [1] role queen
  Row   [1] role row
end

association QueenCol between
  Queen [1] role queen2
  Col   [1] role col
end

------------------------------------------------------------------------

constraints

-- Row/Col index values must themselves form a genuine 0..N-1 permutation
-- (the Set{0,...,N-1} domain given in the .properties file only restricts
-- which VALUES are allowed, it does not by itself force each to be used
-- exactly once) -- these two invariants are what actually pin each
-- Row/Col object to its own distinct board coordinate.
context r1,r2:Row inv distinctRowIdx:
  r1<>r2 implies r1.idx<>r2.idx

context c1,c2:Col inv distinctColIdx:
  c1<>c2 implies c1.idx<>c2.idx

-- The actual N-Queens no-attack condition: two different queens must not
-- share a diagonal in either direction. ("Not the same row" / "not the
-- same column" are already guaranteed structurally by the bijective
-- associations above, so this invariant only needs to rule out the two
-- diagonal directions: q.row.idx - q.col.idx is constant along one
-- diagonal direction, q.row.idx + q.col.idx is constant along the other.)
context q1,q2:Queen inv noAttack:
  q1<>q2 implies
    (q1.row.idx+q1.col.idx <> q2.row.idx+q2.col.idx and
     q1.row.idx-q1.col.idx <> q2.row.idx-q2.col.idx)
